.TH "azure-nvme-id" "8" "October 30, 2026" "azure-nvme-id @VERSION@" "User Manual"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
azure-nvme-id - identify Azure NVMe devices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]azure-nvme-id\f[R] [--debug] [--format {plain|json}] [--help |
--version | --udev]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\f[B]azure-nvme-id\f[R] provides identification metadata in the response
to Identify Namespace command for some models of NVMe devices.
This is found in vendor-specific (vs) field which contains various
identification details with a comma-separated, key=value format.
.PP
\f[B]azure-nvme-id\f[R] combines this metadata with the make and model
of NVMe device namespaces to identify a device by type, name, index,
etc.
.PP
Diagnostics, including underlying system errors, are written to stderr.
.SH DEVICE ENUMERATION
.PP
Without \f[CB]--udev\f[R], \f[B]azure-nvme-id\f[R] enumerates Microsoft
NVMe controllers under \f[CB]/sys/class/nvme\f[R] and identifies their
namespaces.
Output options are plain or json.
.PP
JSON output includes \f[CB]path\f[R], \f[CB]nsid\f[R], \f[CB]model\f[R],
\f[CB]properties\f[R], and \f[CB]vs\f[R] for each namespace.
.PP
These errors are reported to stderr but do not stop enumeration:
.IP \[bu] 2
Missing \f[CB]/sys/class/nvme\f[R] means no controllers.
A missing controller directory means no namespaces for that controller.
.IP \[bu] 2
An unreadable \f[CB]device/vendor\f[R] or \f[CB]model\f[R] file, or an
invalid vendor ID, skips the controller.
.IP \[bu] 2
A failed model reread during fallback identification leaves properties
empty.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CB]lun\f[R] or \f[CB]index\f[R] values that cannot be converted to
integers stay strings in JSON output.
.PP
Exit status \f[CB]0\f[R] does not guarantee that every device was
identified.
.SH UDEV DEVICE IDENTIFICATION
.PP
The \f[CB]--udev\f[R] option identifies a single NVMe namespace device
specified by the required \f[CB]DEVNAME\f[R] environment variable instead
of enumerating controllers.
It queries that device directly, without sysfs vendor filtering or
model-based fallback identification.
.PP
Output is a set of \f[CB]<key>=<value>\f[R] variables for consumption by
udev rules.
\f[CB]AZURE_DISK_VS\f[R] contains the vendor-specific data.
Each property in that data is also printed with its key converted to
uppercase and prefixed with \f[CB]AZURE_DISK_\f[R], such as
\f[CB]AZURE_DISK_TYPE\f[R], \f[CB]AZURE_DISK_INDEX\f[R], and
\f[CB]AZURE_DISK_NAME\f[R].
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[CB]--debug\f[R]
Debug mode with additional logging.
.TP
\f[CB]--format {plain|json}\f[R]
Output format, default is plain.
.TP
\f[CB]--help\f[R]
Show usage information and exit.
.TP
\f[CB]--udev\f[R]
Run in udev mode.
See UDEV DEVICE IDENTIFICATION.
.TP
\f[CB]--version\f[R]
Show version information and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\f[CB]0\f[R]
Success, including \f[CB]--help\f[R], \f[CB]--version\f[R], or finding no
matching devices.
.TP
\f[CB]1\f[R]
Invalid command-line arguments.
.TP
\f[CB]2\f[R]
Failure to scan \f[CB]/sys/class/nvme\f[R], except for \f[CB]ENOENT\f[R],
which is treated as success.
.TP
\f[CB]3\f[R]
Failure to scan \f[CB]/sys/class/nvme/<controller>\f[R] for namespaces,
except for \f[CB]ENOENT\f[R], which is treated as success.
.TP
\f[CB]4\f[R]
Memory allocation failure in namespace identification, udev property
processing, or JSON processing, including JSON construction and
serialization.
.TP
\f[CB]5\f[R]
Failure to open an NVMe namespace device, either to obtain its namespace
ID or to execute Identify Namespace.
.TP
\f[CB]6\f[R]
An ioctl error executing the NVMe Identify Namespace command.
.TP
\f[CB]7\f[R]
The NVMe Identify Namespace command returned a nonzero NVMe completion
status.
The status is printed in hexadecimal on stderr; it is not used directly
as the process exit status.
.TP
\f[CB]8\f[R]
An ioctl error obtaining a namespace ID with \f[CB]NVME_IOCTL_ID\f[R].
.TP
\f[CB]9\f[R]
Failure to obtain the page size with \f[CB]sysconf()\f[R] for Identify
Namespace.
.TP
\f[CB]10\f[R]
Malformed vendor-specific key-value properties in udev mode.
.TP
\f[CB]11\f[R]
The \f[CB]DEVNAME\f[R] environment variable is not set in udev mode.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Identify NVMe namespaces:
.IP
.nf
\f[CR]
$ sudo azure-nvme-id
/dev/nvme0n1:
/dev/nvme1n1: type=local,index=1,name=nvme-110G-1
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Parse device identifiers for udev consumption:
.IP
.nf
\f[CR]
$ sudo env DEVNAME=/dev/nvme1n1 azure-nvme-id --udev
AZURE_DISK_VS=type=local,index=1,name=nvme-110G-1
AZURE_DISK_TYPE=local
AZURE_DISK_INDEX=1
AZURE_DISK_NAME=nvme-110G-1
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Check \f[CB]azure-nvme-id\f[R] version:
.IP
.nf
\f[CR]
$ azure-nvme-id --version
azure-nvme-id 0.1.2
\f[R]
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
Source and documentation available at:
<https://github.com/Azure/azure-vm-utils>
